import TestimonyArguments.SolaGratia.Dispute

/-!
# TestimonyArguments.SolaGratia.Perseverance — the perseverance of the saints

"My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me. I give them eternal
life, and they will never perish, and no one will snatch them out of my hand"
(John 10:27–28). If the grace that saves brings a person to believe, can the one
it saves lose it again? The Westminster Confession of 1647 answers that those
"whom God hath accepted in his Beloved, effectually called and sanctified by his
Spirit, can neither totally nor finally fall away from the state of grace; but
shall certainly persevere therein to the end, and be eternally saved" (XVII.1).
To be *effectually called* is to be called in a way that brings one to faith.
The Synod of Dort had said the same in its Fifth Head of Doctrine in 1619: God
"does not wholly withdraw the Holy Spirit from his own people, even in their
melancholy falls" (V, article 6). This is the *perseverance of the saints*.

## Two claims, and the claim that joins them

Westminster's sentence is about the effectually called, and its tenth chapter
makes them the elect, those God chose to save. Of the elect, Augustine had said
the same about twelve centuries earlier. Those "called according to God's
purpose" are elected, and those who are not to persevere "are not elected" (*On
Rebuke and Grace*, ch. 16). Aquinas says that predestination "most certainly and
infallibly takes effect" (*Summa* I q.23 a.6), and the Formula of Concord that
God placed the elect's salvation "in the almighty hand of our Savior Jesus
Christ, from which no one can pluck us, John 10:28" (Solid Declaration XI.45).
So Augustine, Aquinas and the Lutherans agree with Westminster that the elect
persevere, and divide from it over who the elect are. Trent and Wesley are not
encoded as taking a side on the elect.

Augustine held that some who are truly *regenerate*, given new life by the
Spirit, are not elect: "to some of His own children — whom He has regenerated in
Christ — to whom He has given faith, hope, and love, God does not give
perseverance also" (ch. 18). The Reformed hold the contrary. Everyone truly
justified, that is, put right with God by faith, is among the elect, and those
who fall away never had justifying faith. Among the texts they cite is 1 John
2:19: "They went out from us, but they were not of us; for if they had been of
us, they would have continued with us." Calvin puts it so on Hebrews 6: "God
indeed favours none but the elect alone with the Spirit of regeneration".

So the Reformed conclusion is written as two claims: the elect persevere, and no
one truly justified can finally fall away. A third claim joins them, that the
truly justified are elect. If "the perseverance of the saints" were one claim,
"saints" would have to mean both the elect and everyone truly justified.
Augustine agrees about the first and denies the second, so as one claim he would
seem to contradict Westminster even where he agrees with it.

## The two routes, and where each is disputed

**John 10 and 1 John 2.** From John 10:27–29, the Reformed conclude that the
elect persevere. Calvin reads the verse so: "the salvation of all the elect is
not less certain than the power of God is invincible". Wesley grants the text and
reads its promise as conditional, quoting 2 Peter 1:10: "They are *my sheep*,
that *hear my voice*, that *follow me* in all holiness. And 'if ye do these
things, ye shall never fall'". So the step is rated *disputed*, which means that
a reader the library cites grants its grounds and denies its conclusion. From 1
John 2:19, the Reformed conclude that the truly justified are elect. Augustine
grants the verse and still denies the step: those who left "were not of the
number of children, even when they were in the faith of children" (ch. 20). They
were not elect, and their faith was real. That step is rated *disputed* too.

**Philippians 1.** "He who began a good work in you will bring it to completion
at the day of Jesus Christ" (Philippians 1:6). Trent's chapter on perseverance
first says that "all ought to place and repose a most firm hope in God's help",
and then quotes the verse with a condition: "For God, unless men be themselves
wanting to His grace, as he has begun the good work, so will he perfect it"
(Session VI, ch. 13). So the step from the verse to "no one truly justified can
finally fall away" is rated *disputed*.

The John route reaches the conclusion without Philippians, and the Philippians
route reaches it without 1 John 2:19, though it still needs John 10 for the
elect (`john_strand_establishes`, `philippians_strand_establishes`).

## Augustine, Trent, the Lutherans and Wesley

Four positions hold that one truly justified can finally fall away.

- **Augustine and Aquinas**, on Augustine's ground that some regenerate are not
  given perseverance. They hold that the elect persevere as firmly as
  Westminster does. Augustine's claim, his grant that the elect persevere, and
  the Reformed step from 1 John 2:19 cannot all be held together, and the step
  is where they part (`whereAugustinePartsFromJohnsStrand`).
- **Trent**, from the condition in its chapter 13. Its canon 23 condemns anyone
  who says "that a man once justified can sin no more, nor lose grace, and that
  therefore he that falls and sins was never truly justified". The canon is
  about losing grace; that it can be lost finally is what the condition says.
- **The Lutherans**, from the Formula of Concord. They hold that the elect
  persevere, citing John 10:28, and that some in whom God began the good work
  fall away by their own fault: it "is not as though God were unwilling to
  grant grace for perseverance to those in whom He has begun the good work"
  (Solid Declaration XI.42).
- **Wesley**, from the condition he reads in John 10 and from Hebrews 6:4–6,
  "it is impossible, in the case of those who have once been enlightened … and
  then have fallen away, to restore them again to repentance". He takes it for
  true believers who fell. "The sum of all is this. If the Scriptures are true,
  those who are holy or righteous in the judgment of God himself … may
  nevertheless so fall from God, as to perish everlastingly" (*Serious Thoughts
  upon the Perseverance of the Saints*, §30). The Reformed answer, with Calvin,
  is that those described had "some taste of his grace", a knowledge "which
  afterwards vanishes away", and were never regenerate.

Trent, Wesley and the Lutherans each make the promise hold unless the believer
turns away: Trent "unless men be themselves wanting to His grace", Wesley "if ye
do these things, ye shall never fall", and the Formula of Concord that those who
fall away do so by their own fault. Taken as a condition that can fail, that
simply is the claim that the justified can finally fall away. Taken in the sense
of Dort's Fifth Head, as a condition God keeps in his own sheep ("God is
faithful, who having conferred grace, mercifully confirms and powerfully
preserves them therein, even to the end", V, article 3), it is something Dort
holds. Both senses are checked against Wesley's position, because he has a
second ground besides the condition, Hebrews 6, and his conclusion does not
stand or fall with how the condition is taken (`whatTheConditionMeans`). Wesley
parts from Dort either way. In Dort's sense of the condition he parts at Calvin's
reading of Hebrews 6. In his own sense he parts at the step from John 10, the
step he himself disputes.

## The Remonstrants left it open

The Remonstrants, the Dutch followers of Arminius whose Remonstrance of 1610 the
Synod of Dort answered, did not deny perseverance. Their fifth article says that
Christ, "if only they are ready for the conflict, and desire his help, and are
not inactive, keeps them from falling", so that they cannot be "plucked out of
Christ's hands" (John 10:28). Then it stops: "But whether they are capable,
through negligence, of forsaking again the first beginnings of their life in
Christ … that must be more particularly determined out of the Holy Scripture,
before we ourselves can teach it with the full persuasion of our minds."
Arminius wrote, "I never taught that a true believer can either totally or
finally fall away from the faith, and perish; yet I will not conceal, that there
are passages of Scripture which seem to me to wear this aspect" (*Declaration of
Sentiments*). What the article concludes from John 10:28 leaves the question
open (`remonstrance_leaves_perseverance_open`).

## Weighed together

Weighed as one dispute, Westminster stands off against each of the four
positions that hold the justified can fall away: each defeats the other, so
between them it is a tie. The four do not attack one another. However the ties
are settled, the one thing that must be accepted is what the Remonstrants
concluded from John 10:28: no power from outside snatches believers out of
Christ's hand (`only_the_remonstrance_prevails`). Westminster and each of the
four can be defended, which means each is accepted on at least one way of
settling the ties, and none of the five must be accepted on every way. Every tie
rests on a reading or step rated *disputed*, so the verdict moves only if a new,
cited argument moves one of those ratings.

## What is not encoded

Romans 8:29–30 ("those whom he justified he also glorified") is the third text
most often cited for perseverance, and Hebrews 10:26–29 and 2 Peter 2:20–21 the
texts most often set beside Hebrews 6 against it. None is encoded: no reading
of them was checked against a cited reader with a verified location. Nor is the
certainty of persevering to the end, which Dort grants to believers "according
to the measure of their faith" (V, article 9) and Trent's canon 16 denies except
by special revelation. Nor is falling for a time. Westminster denies that the
elect fall away "totally", and the Smalcald Articles, Luther's articles of 1537,
say that when holy men fall into manifest sins, as David did, "faith and the Holy
Ghost has departed from them". This chapter weighs only falling away finally.
Dort itself grants that believers, left to themselves, "could not persevere in a
state of grace" (V, article 3), and that their falling "with respect to
themselves is not only possible, but would undoubtedly happen" (V, article 8).
The claims here are about what God does, which is where the readers part.
-/
namespace TestimonyArguments.SolaGratia

open Testimony Testimony.Logic Testimony.Logic.Framework

/-! ### The conclusion -/

/-- **The perseverance of the saints**, as two claims: the elect persevere, and
no one truly justified can finally fall away. The first, Augustine, Aquinas and
the Lutherans hold as firmly as Westminster. The second is where they part. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def perseverance : Formula Claim :=
  ⋀ [p .electPersevere, notP .justifiedCanFinallyFallAway]

/-! ### The steps -/

/-- John 10:27–29: those the Father gave to Christ "will never perish". So the
elect persevere. Rated `disputed`: Wesley grants the text and reads its promise
as conditional, quoting 2 Peter 1:10. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def john10Step : Formula Claim :=
  p .john10_27to29 ➝ p .electPersevere

/-- 1 John 2:19: "if they had been of us, they would have continued with us". So
everyone truly justified is among the elect, and those who left were never truly
justified.
Rated `disputed`: Augustine grants the verse and holds that those who left had
true faith and were not elect. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def bridgeStep : Formula Claim :=
  p .firstJohn2_19 ➝ p .justifiedAreElect

/-- If the elect persevere and the truly justified are elect, no one truly
justified can finally fall away. Rated `consensus`: it follows from what the
words mean. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def joinStep : Formula Claim :=
  ⋀ [p .electPersevere, p .justifiedAreElect] ➝ notP .justifiedCanFinallyFallAway

/-- Philippians 1:6: he who began a good work will bring it to completion. So no
one truly justified can finally fall away. Rated `disputed`: Trent quotes the
verse with "unless men be themselves wanting to His grace". -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def philippiansStep : Formula Claim :=
  p .philippians1_6 ➝ notP .justifiedCanFinallyFallAway

/-- If God's promise holds unless men are wanting to his grace, one truly
justified can finally fall away. Rated `wellSupported`. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def conditionalStep : Formula Claim :=
  p .promiseIsConditional ➝ p .justifiedCanFinallyFallAway

/-- Hebrews 6:4–6, read as describing true believers who fall away: so one truly
justified can finally fall away. Rated `wellSupported`; the dissent is from the
reading. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def hebrewsStep : Formula Claim :=
  ⋀ [p .hebrews6_4to6, p .hebrews6DescribesBelieversWhoFall] ➝
    p .justifiedCanFinallyFallAway

/-- If some whom God has regenerated are not given perseverance, one truly
justified can finally fall away. Rated `wellSupported`. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def augustineStep : Formula Claim :=
  p .someRegenerateNotGivenPerseverance ➝ p .justifiedCanFinallyFallAway

/-- John 10:28: no one will snatch them out of Christ's hand. So no power from
outside snatches believers out of it. Rated `consensus`. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def remonstranceStep : Formula Claim :=
  p .john10_27to29 ➝ p .noneSnatchedByForce

/-! ### The lines -/

/-- **John 10:27–29**: the elect persevere. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def john10Line : Line Claim :=
  { name := "John 10:27–29 (\"no one will snatch them out of my hand\")"
  , grounds := [p .john10_27to29]
  , step := john10Step
  , delivers := p .electPersevere
  , inference := some john10Inference }

/-- **1 John 2:19**: the truly justified are elect. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def bridgeLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "1 John 2:19 (\"they went out from us, but they were not of us\")"
  , grounds := [p .firstJohn2_19]
  , step := bridgeStep
  , delivers := p .justifiedAreElect
  , inference := some bridgeInference }

/-- **Philippians 1:6**: no one truly justified can finally fall away. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def philippiansLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Philippians 1:6 (\"he who began a good work in you will bring it to completion\")"
  , grounds := [p .philippians1_6]
  , step := philippiansStep
  , delivers := notP .justifiedCanFinallyFallAway
  , inference := some philippiansInference }

/-- **The condition**: the promise holds unless men are wanting to God's grace. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def conditionalLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "The promise, kept \"unless men be themselves wanting to His grace\""
  , grounds := [p .promiseIsConditional]
  , step := conditionalStep
  , delivers := p .justifiedCanFinallyFallAway
  , inference := some conditionalInference }

/-- **Hebrews 6:4–6**, read as describing true believers who fall away. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def hebrewsLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Hebrews 6:4–6, as describing true believers who fall away"
  , grounds := [p .hebrews6_4to6, p .hebrews6DescribesBelieversWhoFall]
  , step := hebrewsStep
  , delivers := p .justifiedCanFinallyFallAway
  , inference := some hebrewsInference }

/-- **Augustine**: some regenerate are not given perseverance. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def augustineLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Some regenerate are not given perseverance (Augustine)"
  , grounds := [p .someRegenerateNotGivenPerseverance]
  , step := augustineStep
  , delivers := p .justifiedCanFinallyFallAway
  , inference := some augustineInference }

/-- **The Remonstrance, article 5**: no one plucks believers out of Christ's
hand. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def remonstranceLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Remonstrance, article 5 (John 10:28)"
  , grounds := [p .john10_27to29]
  , step := remonstranceStep
  , delivers := p .noneSnatchedByForce
  , inference := some remonstranceInference }

/-! ### The positions -/

/-- The label every position asked for the falling away shares. -/
private def fallingAwayLabel : String :=
  "one who is truly justified can fall away from grace finally, and perish"

/-- **The Remonstrants of 1610**: what their fifth article concludes from John
10:28, that no one plucks believers out of Christ's hand. The article puts a
condition on Christ's keeping, "if only they are ready for the conflict, and
desire his help, and are not inactive". That is the condition the dilemma below
takes two ways, and the article does not say which way it means, so it is not
carried here as a premise. It leaves whether believers can fall away by
negligence to be "more particularly determined out of the Holy Scripture". -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def remonstrance : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { name := "Remonstrance (article 5, 1610)"
  , cite := remonstrantCite
  , premises := caseOf [remonstranceLine] [] []
  , conclusion := p .noneSnatchedByForce
  , conclusionLabel := "no power from outside snatches believers out of Christ's hand"
  , inferences := [remonstranceInference] }

/-- **Augustine and Aquinas.** The elect persevere; and, on Augustine's ground, to
some whom God has regenerated he does not give perseverance, so one truly
justified can finally fall away. They read 1 John 2:19 as Augustine does, of those who were in the
faith and were not elect. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def augustinian : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { name := "Augustine and Aquinas (On Rebuke and Grace; Summa I q.23)"
  , cite := augustinianCite
  , premises := caseOf [augustineLine] [p .electPersevere, p .firstJohn2_19] []
  , conclusion := ⋀ [p .electPersevere, p .justifiedCanFinallyFallAway]
  , conclusionLabel := "the elect persevere, and some truly justified who are not elect fall away"
  , inferences := [augustineInference] }

/-- **Trent**, from the sixth session (1547): Philippians 1:6, as its thirteenth
chapter reads it, with the condition "unless men be themselves wanting to His
grace". -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def trentOnPerseverance : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { name := "Trent (Session VI, chapter 13 and canon 23)"
  , cite := tridentineCite
  , premises := caseOf [conditionalLine] [p .philippians1_6] []
  , conclusion := p .justifiedCanFinallyFallAway
  , conclusionLabel := fallingAwayLabel
  , inferences := [conditionalInference] }

/-- **The Lutherans**, from the Formula of Concord (1577). The elect persevere,
"from which no one can pluck us, John 10:28" (Solid Declaration XI.45); and those
in whom God began the good work can fall away by their own fault (XI.42). -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def lutheranOnPerseverance : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { name := "Lutheran (the Formula of Concord, XI)"
  , cite := lutheranCite
  , premises := caseOf [conditionalLine] [p .john10_27to29, p .electPersevere] []
  , conclusion := ⋀ [p .electPersevere, p .justifiedCanFinallyFallAway]
  , conclusionLabel := "the elect persevere, and some in whom God began the good work fall away"
  , inferences := [conditionalInference] }

/-- **Wesley**, from *Serious Thoughts upon the Perseverance of the Saints*
(1751). John 10:27–29, with the condition he reads in it, "if ye do these
things, ye shall never fall" (2 Peter 1:10); and Hebrews 6:4–6, read as describing true
believers who fell. The condition is carried as Wesley states it, with no step
drawn from it, because what it commits him to is what the dilemma below asks.
His conclusion comes from Hebrews 6. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def wesleyOnPerseverance : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { name := "Wesley (Serious Thoughts upon the Perseverance of the Saints)"
  , cite := wesleyanCite
  , premises := caseOf [hebrewsLine] [p .john10_27to29, p .promiseIsConditional] []
  , conclusion := p .justifiedCanFinallyFallAway
  , conclusionLabel := fallingAwayLabel
  , inferences := [hebrewsInference] }

/-! ### The position argued for -/

/-- The Reformed answer to Hebrews 6, with Calvin: those described had only
"some taste" of grace, and were never regenerate. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def perseveranceShared : List (Formula Claim) :=
  [p .hebrews6_4to6, notP .hebrews6DescribesBelieversWhoFall]

/-- **The Reformed position**, as Westminster (1647) and Dort (1619) state it.
Two routes to the claim that no one truly justified can finally fall away: John
10:27–29 with 1 John 2:19, joined; and Philippians 1:6. With Calvin's answer to
Hebrews 6. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def perseveranceReformed : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { name := "Reformed (Westminster XVII; Dort, Fifth Head)"
  , cite := reformedCite
  , premises :=
      caseOf [john10Line, bridgeLine, philippiansLine] perseveranceShared [joinStep]
  , conclusion := perseverance
  , conclusionLabel :=
      "the perseverance of the saints: the elect persevere, and no one truly justified " ++
      "can finally fall away"
  , inferences := [john10Inference, bridgeInference, philippiansInference, joinInference] }

/-- The John strand alone: John 10:27–29 and 1 John 2:19, joined. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def johnStrand : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { perseveranceReformed with
    name := "Reformed, the John strand alone"
    premises := caseOf [john10Line, bridgeLine] perseveranceShared [joinStep]
    inferences := [john10Inference, bridgeInference, joinInference] }

/-- The Philippians strand alone, with John 10:27–29 for the elect. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def philippiansStrand : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { perseveranceReformed with
    name := "Reformed, the Philippians strand alone"
    premises := caseOf [john10Line, philippiansLine] perseveranceShared []
    inferences := [john10Inference, philippiansInference] }

/-! ### Readings -/

/-- The Reformed reading: no one truly justified falls away finally; Hebrews 6
describes those who had only a taste of grace; and no regenerate person is left
without perseverance. The promise is not conditional on anything God does not
himself keep. -/
def perseveringReading : Valuation Claim := fun a =>
  match a with
  | .justifiedCanFinallyFallAway => False
  | .hebrews6DescribesBelieversWhoFall => False
  | .someRegenerateNotGivenPerseverance => False
  | .promiseIsConditional => False
  | _ => True

/-- A reading on which every rival's premises hold: the elect persevere, and some
truly justified, not being elect, finally fall away. Not a position the rivals
share: Wesley, for one, is not encoded as holding that the elect persevere. -/
def fallingAwayReading : Valuation Claim := fun a =>
  match a with
  | .justifiedAreElect => False
  | _ => True

/-! ### What follows -/

/-- **The Reformed premises establish the perseverance of the saints**: the
elect persevere, and no one truly justified can finally fall away. That the
second follows rests on readings and steps rated `disputed`: the steps from
John 10 and 1 John 2, and from Philippians 1:6. -/
@[headline]
theorem perseveranceReformed_establishes : Establishes perseveranceReformed := by
  establish [solaGratiaDefs]

#print axioms perseveranceReformed_establishes

/-- The Reformed premises can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem perseveranceReformed_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable perseveranceReformed.premises := by
  satisfied_by perseveringReading [solaGratiaDefs]

/-- **The John strand reaches the conclusion alone.** John 10:27–29 for the
elect, 1 John 2:19 for the truly justified being elect, and the two joined. -/
@[headline]
theorem john_strand_establishes : Establishes johnStrand := by
  establish [solaGratiaDefs]

#print axioms john_strand_establishes

/-- The John strand can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem johnStrand_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable johnStrand.premises := by
  satisfied_by perseveringReading [solaGratiaDefs]

/-- **The Philippians strand reaches the conclusion alone**, without 1 John 2:19:
Philippians 1:6 for the truly justified, and John 10:27–29 for the elect. -/
@[headline]
theorem philippians_strand_establishes : Establishes philippiansStrand := by
  establish [solaGratiaDefs]

#print axioms philippians_strand_establishes

/-- The Philippians strand can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem philippiansStrand_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable philippiansStrand.premises := by
  satisfied_by perseveringReading [solaGratiaDefs]

/-- The Remonstrance's conclusion follows from its premises. -/
theorem remonstrance_establishes : Establishes remonstrance := by
  establish [solaGratiaDefs]

/-- The Remonstrance can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem remonstrance_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable remonstrance.premises := by
  satisfied_by perseveringReading [solaGratiaDefs]

/-- **The Remonstrants' premises leave perseverance open.** Their fifth article
grants from John 10:28 that no one plucks believers out of Christ's hand, and
leaves whether they can fall away by negligence to be "more particularly
determined out of the Holy Scripture". The Reformed reading satisfies it, and so
does a reading on which every rival's premises hold. So the article neither teaches the
perseverance of the saints nor denies it, as Arminius said of himself: "I never
taught that a true believer can either totally or finally fall away from the
faith, and perish". -/
@[headline]
theorem remonstrance_leaves_perseverance_open :
    Independent remonstrance.premises (p .justifiedCanFinallyFallAway) := by
  leaves_open perseveringReading fallingAwayReading [solaGratiaDefs]

#print axioms remonstrance_leaves_perseverance_open

/-- Augustine and Aquinas's conclusion follows from their premises. -/
theorem augustinian_establishes : Establishes augustinian := by
  establish [solaGratiaDefs]

/-- Augustine and Aquinas can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem augustinian_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable augustinian.premises := by
  satisfied_by fallingAwayReading [solaGratiaDefs]

/-- Trent's conclusion follows from its premises. -/
theorem trentOnPerseverance_establishes : Establishes trentOnPerseverance := by
  establish [solaGratiaDefs]

/-- Trent can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem trentOnPerseverance_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable trentOnPerseverance.premises := by
  satisfied_by fallingAwayReading [solaGratiaDefs]

/-- The Lutherans' conclusion follows from their premises. -/
theorem lutheranOnPerseverance_establishes : Establishes lutheranOnPerseverance := by
  establish [solaGratiaDefs]

/-- The Lutherans can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem lutheranOnPerseverance_is_satisfiable :
    Satisfiable lutheranOnPerseverance.premises := by
  satisfied_by fallingAwayReading [solaGratiaDefs]

/-- Wesley's conclusion follows from his premises. -/
theorem wesleyOnPerseverance_establishes : Establishes wesleyOnPerseverance := by
  establish [solaGratiaDefs]

/-- Wesley can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem wesleyOnPerseverance_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable wesleyOnPerseverance.premises := by
  satisfied_by fallingAwayReading [solaGratiaDefs]

/-! ### Where Augustine parts from Westminster -/

/-- **Where Augustine parts from the John strand: at 1 John 2:19.** Augustine
grants that the elect persevere, and the Reformed step from 1 John 2:19 joins
the elect to every one truly justified. Augustine's claim that some regenerate
are not given perseverance, his grant that the elect persevere, and that step
cannot all be held together, and the crux is the step. It is rated `disputed`, and the reader
who disputes it is Augustine himself: those who left "were not of the number of
children, even when they were in the faith of children". -/
def whereAugustinePartsFromJohnsStrand : Because johnStrand augustinian :=
  Because.ofChecks bridgeStep
    ([p .john10_27to29, p .firstJohn2_19] ++ perseveranceShared ++ [john10Step]) [joinStep]
    [p .firstJohn2_19, joinStep]
    [p .someRegenerateNotGivenPerseverance, augustineStep, p .electPersevere]
    .derives john_strand_establishes johnStrand_is_satisfiable
    (by simp [johnStrand, caseOf, solaGratiaDefs])
    (by simp [johnStrand, caseOf, solaGratiaDefs])
    (by simp [augustinian, caseOf, solaGratiaDefs])
    (by decide +kernel)

/-- **Where Augustine's position meets the Philippians route: at its one step.**
Augustine is not cited on Philippians 1:6. The step from the verse to "no one
truly justified can finally fall away" is rated `disputed` because Trent quotes
the verse with its condition, and the Formula of Concord cites it while
explaining why some fall away. -/
def whereAugustinePartsFromPhilippians : Because philippiansStrand augustinian :=
  Because.ofChecks philippiansStep
    ([p .john10_27to29, p .philippians1_6] ++ perseveranceShared ++ [john10Step]) []
    [p .philippians1_6]
    [p .someRegenerateNotGivenPerseverance, augustineStep]
    .derives philippians_strand_establishes philippiansStrand_is_satisfiable
    (by simp [philippiansStrand, caseOf, solaGratiaDefs])
    (by simp [philippiansStrand, caseOf, solaGratiaDefs])
    (by simp [augustinian, caseOf, solaGratiaDefs])
    (by decide +kernel)

/-! ### What the condition means -/

/-- The condition read as one God himself keeps: those who are Christ's sheep do
hear and follow, because God keeps them in faith. Dort's reading. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def asConditionGodKeeps : Reading Claim :=
  { name := "as a condition God himself keeps in his sheep, Dort's sense"
  , commits := p .godKeepsHisSheepInFaith
  , source := asConditionGodKeepsSource }

/-- The condition read as one that can fail: one truly justified can fall away
finally. Wesley's sense. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def asConditionThatCanFail : Reading Claim :=
  { name := "as a condition that can fail, Wesley's sense"
  , commits := p .justifiedCanFinallyFallAway
  , source := asConditionThatCanFailSource }

/-- Wesley, with the condition read in Dort's sense. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def wesleyAsGodKeeps : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  wesleyOnPerseverance.readAs (p .promiseIsConditional) asConditionGodKeeps

/-- Wesley, with the condition read in his own sense. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def wesleyAsCanFail : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  wesleyOnPerseverance.readAs (p .promiseIsConditional) asConditionThatCanFail

/-- Wesley read the first way can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem wesleyAsGodKeeps_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable wesleyAsGodKeeps.premises := by
  satisfied_by fallingAwayReading [solaGratiaDefs]

/-- Wesley read the second way can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem wesleyAsCanFail_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable wesleyAsCanFail.premises := by
  satisfied_by fallingAwayReading [solaGratiaDefs]

/-- **Why Westminster stands against Wesley, even with the condition read in
Dort's sense.** Wesley's reading of Hebrews 6:4–6 still delivers that one truly
justified can finally fall away. The crux is Calvin's reading of the passage,
that those described had only "some taste of his grace". It is Westminster's
answer to Wesley, rated `disputed`. -/
def whereWesleysHebrewsFalls : Because perseveranceReformed wesleyAsGodKeeps :=
  Because.ofChecks (notP .hebrews6DescribesBelieversWhoFall)
    [p .john10_27to29, p .firstJohn2_19, p .philippians1_6, p .hebrews6_4to6]
    [john10Step, bridgeStep, philippiansStep, joinStep]
    []
    [p .hebrews6DescribesBelieversWhoFall]
    .answers perseveranceReformed_establishes perseveranceReformed_is_satisfiable
    (by simp [perseveranceReformed, caseOf, solaGratiaDefs])
    (by simp)
    (by simp [solaGratiaDefs, caseOf])
    (by decide +kernel)

/-- **Why the John route stands against Wesley, with the condition taken as he
means it.** Then the condition says that one truly justified can finally fall
away. The John route needs both its steps to deny that, and the crux is the
step from John 10:27–29, the one Wesley himself disputes (§18). -/
def whereWesleysConditionFalls : Because johnStrand wesleyAsCanFail :=
  Because.ofChecks john10Step
    ([p .john10_27to29, p .firstJohn2_19] ++ perseveranceShared) [bridgeStep, joinStep]
    [p .john10_27to29, p .firstJohn2_19, bridgeStep, joinStep]
    [p .promiseIsConditional, p .promiseIsConditional ➝ p .justifiedCanFinallyFallAway]
    .derives john_strand_establishes johnStrand_is_satisfiable
    (by simp [johnStrand, caseOf, solaGratiaDefs])
    (by simp [johnStrand, caseOf, solaGratiaDefs])
    (by simp [solaGratiaDefs, caseOf])
    (by decide +kernel)

/-- **What the condition means, read both ways.** The claim is the one Trent,
the Lutherans and Wesley share: God's promise to keep those he has begun to save
holds unless the believer turns away. Both senses are checked against Wesley's
position, because he has a second ground besides the condition, Hebrews 6, so
his conclusion does not stand or fall with how the condition is taken.

Taken as a condition God himself keeps, in the sense of Dort's Fifth Head
(article 3), the condition is something Dort holds. Wesley still cannot be held
with Westminster, because his reading of Hebrews 6:4–6 delivers the falling away
by itself; the crux is Calvin's reading of that passage.

Taken as a condition that can fail, which is how Wesley means it ("a Saint may
fall away"), the condition is the falling away. It cannot be held with the John
route, at the step from John 10:27–29, which Wesley disputes.

Each reading is consistent: Wesley read either way can be held without
contradiction. Both breaks come at readings and steps rated `disputed`, so
neither shows the other side right. -/
def whatTheConditionMeans : Dilemma wesleyOnPerseverance where
  claim := p .promiseIsConditional
  horns :=
    [ { reading := asConditionGodKeeps
      , fair := wesleyAsGodKeeps_is_satisfiable
      , fates := [.falls perseveranceReformed whereWesleysHebrewsFalls]
      , answered := by simp }
    , { reading := asConditionThatCanFail
      , fair := wesleyAsCanFail_is_satisfiable
      , fates := [.falls johnStrand whereWesleysConditionFalls]
      , answered := by simp } ]
  claim_mem := by simp [solaGratiaDefs, caseOf]
  two := by simp

/-! ### The positions weighed

Six parties are heard: the Remonstrance, with what it concludes from John 10:28;
Westminster and Dort; Augustine and Aquinas; Trent; the Lutherans; and Wesley.
One position defeats another when it contradicts something the other holds and
its weakest premise or step is rated no lower than the other's. Who defeats whom
is computed from their premises and checked by the kernel. -/

/-- The parties to the dispute over perseverance. -/
inductive PerseveranceParty
  /-- The Remonstrance of 1610: no power from outside snatches believers out of
  Christ's hand. Its weakest link is `consensus`. -/
  | remonstrance
  /-- Westminster and Dort: the elect persevere, and no one truly justified can
  finally fall away. Its weakest link is `disputed`: each step from a text, and
  Calvin's reading of Hebrews 6. -/
  | reformed
  /-- Augustine and Aquinas: the elect persevere, and some truly justified who
  are not elect fall away. Its weakest link is `disputed`: that some regenerate
  are not given perseverance. -/
  | augustinian
  /-- Trent: the promise is kept unless men are wanting to God's grace. Its
  weakest link is `disputed`: that condition. -/
  | trent
  /-- The Lutherans: the elect persevere, and some in whom God began the good
  work fall away. Its weakest link is `disputed`: the condition. -/
  | lutheran
  /-- Wesley: Hebrews 6:4–6 describes true believers who fall away. Its weakest
  link is `disputed`: that reading. -/
  | wesleyan
deriving DecidableEq

/-- The package each party argues from. -/
@[solaGratiaDefs]
def perseveranceNode : PerseveranceParty → ArgumentPackage Claim
  | .remonstrance => remonstrance
  | .reformed => perseveranceReformed
  | .augustinian => augustinian
  | .trent => trentOnPerseverance
  | .lutheran => lutheranOnPerseverance
  | .wesleyan => wesleyOnPerseverance

/-- The dispute over the perseverance of the saints: every party can be held
without contradiction, delivers its conclusion, and has its steps rated. -/
declare_dispute perseveranceDispute := perseveranceNode using solaGratiaDefs
  /-- **Who defeats whom**, all 36 pairs: eight defeats, four standoffs.
  Westminster stands off against Augustine and Aquinas, Trent, the Lutherans and
  Wesley, over whether one truly justified can finally fall away. The four do
  not attack one another, and nothing attacks the Remonstrance. So each of the
  four can be defended alongside the other three, and none must be accepted,
  because Westminster defeats each of them. Every cell is checked by the
  kernel. -/
  defeats
    [ (.reformed, .augustinian), (.reformed, .trent), (.reformed, .lutheran)
    , (.reformed, .wesleyan)
    , (.augustinian, .reformed), (.trent, .reformed), (.lutheran, .reformed)
    , (.wesleyan, .reformed) ]

/-- How far the dispute is settled before any choice. Nothing attacks the
Remonstrance; every other party has a defeater that the Remonstrance does not
answer. -/
declare_verdict remonstranceUnanswered for perseveranceDispute :
    .groundedExactly [[.remonstrance]]
      [ (.reformed, .augustinian), (.augustinian, .reformed), (.trent, .reformed)
      , (.lutheran, .reformed), (.wesleyan, .reformed) ]
  /-- **Only what the Remonstrants concluded from John 10:28 prevails: no power
  from outside snatches believers out of Christ's hand.** Every other party is
  in a tie with another, and this is all that must be accepted however the ties
  are settled.

  What this does not claim: that the perseverance of the saints is false, or that
  one truly justified can finally fall away. Both are in a standoff, and neither
  is forced. -/
  proves @[headline] only_the_remonstrance_prevails

#print axioms only_the_remonstrance_prevails

/-- Why Westminster can be defended: with the Remonstrance, it answers every
attacker itself. -/
declare_verdict westminsterDefended for perseveranceDispute :
    .credulous .reformed [.remonstrance, .reformed]
  /-- **Westminster can be defended**: on at least one way of settling the ties,
  it is accepted, with the Remonstrance, because it defeats each of the four
  that attack it. -/
  proves @[headline] perseverance_defensible

#print axioms perseverance_defensible

/-- Why Westminster is not forced: a maximal defensible position holds Augustine
and Aquinas, and they defeat Westminster. -/
declare_verdict westminsterAnsweredByAugustine for perseveranceDispute :
    .notSkeptical .reformed .augustinian
      [.remonstrance, .augustinian, .trent, .lutheran, .wesleyan]
  /-- **Nor must Westminster be accepted.** On another way of settling the ties,
  Augustine and Aquinas, Trent, the Lutherans and Wesley are accepted together,
  and Westminster cannot be accepted with them. -/
  proves @[headline] perseverance_not_forced

#print axioms perseverance_not_forced

/-- Why Augustine and Aquinas can be defended: with Trent, the Lutherans and
Wesley, each of whom defeats Westminster. -/
declare_verdict augustineDefended for perseveranceDispute :
    .credulous .augustinian [.remonstrance, .augustinian, .trent, .lutheran, .wesleyan]
  /-- **Augustine and Aquinas can be defended**: accepted together with Trent,
  the Lutherans and Wesley, who agree that one truly justified can finally fall
  away, and each of whom answers Westminster. -/
  proves @[headline] augustinian_defensible

#print axioms augustinian_defensible

/-- Why Augustine and Aquinas are not forced: Westminster defeats them. -/
declare_verdict augustineAnsweredByWestminster for perseveranceDispute :
    .notSkeptical .augustinian .reformed [.remonstrance, .reformed]
  /-- Nor must Augustine and Aquinas be accepted: the way of settling the ties
  that accepts Westminster cannot accept them. -/
  proves @[headline] augustinian_not_forced

#print axioms augustinian_not_forced

/-- Why Trent can be defended on perseverance. -/
declare_verdict trentDefendedOnPerseverance for perseveranceDispute :
    .credulous .trent [.remonstrance, .augustinian, .trent, .lutheran, .wesleyan]
  /-- **Trent can be defended** on perseverance, in the same company. -/
  proves @[headline] trent_on_perseverance_defensible

#print axioms trent_on_perseverance_defensible

/-- Why Trent is not forced on perseverance: Westminster defeats it. -/
declare_verdict trentAnsweredOnPerseverance for perseveranceDispute :
    .notSkeptical .trent .reformed [.remonstrance, .reformed]
  /-- Nor must Trent be accepted, for the same reason. -/
  proves @[headline] trent_on_perseverance_not_forced

#print axioms trent_on_perseverance_not_forced

/-- Why the Lutherans can be defended on perseverance. -/
declare_verdict lutheransDefendedOnPerseverance for perseveranceDispute :
    .credulous .lutheran [.remonstrance, .augustinian, .trent, .lutheran, .wesleyan]
  /-- **The Lutherans can be defended** on perseverance, in the same company. -/
  proves @[headline] lutheran_on_perseverance_defensible

#print axioms lutheran_on_perseverance_defensible

/-- Why the Lutherans are not forced on perseverance: Westminster defeats them. -/
declare_verdict lutheransAnsweredOnPerseverance for perseveranceDispute :
    .notSkeptical .lutheran .reformed [.remonstrance, .reformed]
  /-- Nor must the Lutherans be accepted, for the same reason. -/
  proves @[headline] lutheran_on_perseverance_not_forced

#print axioms lutheran_on_perseverance_not_forced

/-- Why Wesley can be defended on perseverance. -/
declare_verdict wesleyDefendedOnPerseverance for perseveranceDispute :
    .credulous .wesleyan [.remonstrance, .augustinian, .trent, .lutheran, .wesleyan]
  /-- **Wesley can be defended** on perseverance, in the same company. -/
  proves @[headline] wesley_on_perseverance_defensible

#print axioms wesley_on_perseverance_defensible

/-- Why Wesley is not forced on perseverance: Westminster defeats him. -/
declare_verdict wesleyAnsweredOnPerseverance for perseveranceDispute :
    .notSkeptical .wesleyan .reformed [.remonstrance, .reformed]
  /-- Nor must Wesley be accepted, for the same reason. -/
  proves @[headline] wesley_on_perseverance_not_forced

#print axioms wesley_on_perseverance_not_forced

end TestimonyArguments.SolaGratia
